Implement seamless noise. Canvas size 512, use ImageData for speed. Be careful with performance: fbm 4 octaves each pixel 512²*4 ≈1M noise calls * 4 lattice hashes = 4M, okay.

Value noise period wrap:
```js
function makeNoise(seed) {
  const perm = new Uint8Array(512); fill with shuffled 0..255 using rng; 
  function hash(xi,yi){ return perm[(perm[xi&255]+ (yi&255))&255]/255 }
  return function(x,y,period){ ... use xi mod period? }
}
```
Seamless: sample with period; to tile, use lattice coords modulo period. Use hash(xi % period, yi % period). For fbm, period per octave e.g. 8,16,32,64. Since 512 pixels, sample nx = x / size * period; the noise wraps around period. Implement:
```js
function tileNoise(x, y, period, seed) {
  const xi = Math.floor(x), yi=...
  const xf = x-xi, ...
  const u = fade(xf), v=fade(yf)
  const a = hash((xi%period+period)%period, ...)
```
The hash based on integer coords; if lattice loops with period, seamless. Use hash2 with seed.

We'll write:
```js
function hash2(x,y,seed){ let h = x*374761393 + y*668265263 + seed*144269...; h = (h^(h>>>13))*1274126177; return ((h^(h>>>16))>>>0)/4294967295; }
```
JS number precision for big multiplications—use Math.imul to keep 32-bit.
```js
let h = Math.imul(x,374761393) + Math.imul(y,668265263) + Math.imul(seed,1442695041);
h = Math.imul(h ^ (h >>> 13), 1274126177);
h ^= h >>> 16;
return (h>>>0)/4294967295;
```
Use bit ops on negative numbers fine.

fbm:
```js
function fbm(x,y,seed,octaves=4,period=8){
 let amp=.5,freq=period,total=0,norm=0;
 for(i){ total += tileNoise(x*freq, y*freq, freq, seed+i)*amp; ... freq*=2; amp*=.5}
 return total/norm
}
```
where x,y normalized 0..1. tileNoise expects lattice period = freq (points per tile). Good.

